@page "/learn/server-initiated-circuit-pause"
@layout LearnLayout

<PageTitle>Pause circuits from the server · Field Guide</PageTitle>

<article class="l-article" data-learn>
    <h1 class="l-title">Pause circuits from the server</h1>
    <p class="l-sub">You need to restart for a deploy. Can server code ask each tab to save its form first?</p>
    <p class="l-meta">.NET 11 · Interactive Server · about 5 minutes</p>
    <p class="l-new">
        <b>What's new in .NET 11:</b> <code>Circuit.RequestCircuitPauseAsync()</code>. Server code can ask a tab to
        pause, and the call returns straight away. Pausing itself isn't new. In .NET 10 a tab could already pause, save
        its <code>[PersistentState]</code> values, and resume later on a new server. What was missing was a clean way
        for the server to ask.
    </p>

    <p>
        Picture a deploy. Before the server restarts, a script asks every open tab to pause, so each tab saves its
        half-filled form and picks up again on the new server. The script goes through the tabs one at a time:
    </p>

    <figure class="fig anim" data-anim>
        <figcaption class="fig-title">Fig. 1 · .NET 10: a deploy script asks three tabs to pause</figcaption>
        <button type="button" class="fig-replay" data-replay hidden>▶ Play</button>
        <div class="lane">
            <div class="lane-h bad">.NET 10 · ask through JS interop</div>
            <ol class="steps">
                <li>ask tab 1 <span class="wait" style="--d:1.2s">… waiting for a reply</span></li>
                <li class="never" style="--d:3s">ask tab 2</li>
                <li class="never" style="--d:3s">ask tab 3</li>
                <li class="never" style="--d:3s">restart the server</li>
            </ol>
            <div class="tabs">
                <div class="tab paused" style="--d:0.9s"><b>Tab 1</b><span class="was">live</span><span class="now">paused ✓</span></div>
                <div class="tab"><b>Tab 2</b><span>live</span></div>
                <div class="tab"><b>Tab 3</b><span>live</span></div>
            </div>
            <div class="verdict bad" style="--d:3.6s">✗ stuck on tab 1, forever. The restart never runs.</div>
        </div>
        <p class="fig-cap">
            Tab 1 pauses and saves its form, so the pause itself works. But the script is still waiting for tab 1 to
            reply, and no reply ever comes. It never gets to tab 2.
        </p>
    </figure>

    <h2><small>The problem</small>The reply needs the connection the pause closes</h2>
    <p>When server code calls JavaScript, the reply comes back over the tab's connection. The pause closes that connection:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">What happens to the awaited call</span>
        <pre class="snip"><span class="dim">server</span>   await js.InvokeVoidAsync("Blazor.pauseCircuit")  ──▶ <span class="dim">browser</span>
<span class="dim">browser</span>  saves the state, closes the circuit
<span class="dim">browser</span>  the reply ──▶ <span class="err">no connection left to carry it</span>
<span class="dim">server</span>   the await <span class="err">never returns</span>; the code after it never runs</pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        So deploy code that waits on each tab in turn gets stuck on the first one. You can get around it with
        your own script that calls <code>Blazor.pauseCircuit</code> and returns at once. That works, but it's
        JavaScript you ship in every app, and it can't tell you if the tab was still there to ask.
    </p>

    <h2><small>The fix</small>Ask through the Circuit</h2>
    <p>
        When a tab connects, Blazor gives its <code>Circuit</code> to your circuit handler. Keep it, then call the
        new method on it. Only the method is new.
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The whole fix</span>
        <pre class="snip"><span class="dim">// A scoped CircuitHandler is handed its circuit when it opens</span>
public override Task OnCircuitOpenedAsync(Circuit circuit, CancellationToken cancel)
{
    Circuit = circuit;
    return Task.CompletedTask;
}

<span class="dim">// Anywhere on the server</span>
<span class="hl">bool asked = await Tracker.Circuit.RequestCircuitPauseAsync();</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        It sends the request to the browser and returns. The browser then pauses as if it had chosen to.
        <code>true</code> means the request went out, not that the tab has paused yet. <code>false</code> means it
        couldn't ask: the circuit had closed, hadn't finished starting, or had lost its connection.
    </p>

    <figure class="fig anim" data-anim>
        <figcaption class="fig-title">Fig. 2 · .NET 11: the same deploy script, asking through the Circuit</figcaption>
        <button type="button" class="fig-replay" data-replay hidden>▶ Play</button>
        <div class="lane">
            <div class="lane-h ok">.NET 11 · RequestCircuitPauseAsync()</div>
            <ol class="steps">
                <li>ask tab 1 <span class="done" style="--d:0.6s">✓ asked</span></li>
                <li>ask tab 2 <span class="done" style="--d:1.5s">✓ asked</span></li>
                <li>ask tab 3 <span class="done" style="--d:2.4s">✓ asked</span></li>
                <li>restart the server <span class="done" style="--d:3.3s">✓</span></li>
            </ol>
            <div class="tabs">
                <div class="tab paused" style="--d:0.9s"><b>Tab 1</b><span class="was">live</span><span class="now">paused ✓</span></div>
                <div class="tab paused" style="--d:1.8s"><b>Tab 2</b><span class="was">live</span><span class="now">paused ✓</span></div>
                <div class="tab paused" style="--d:2.7s"><b>Tab 3</b><span class="was">live</span><span class="now">paused ✓</span></div>
            </div>
            <div class="verdict ok" style="--d:3.6s">✓ every tab asked, and the server restarts.</div>
        </div>
        <p class="fig-cap">
            Each call returns as soon as the request goes out, so the script moves straight on to the next tab. Every
            tab saves its form, and the restart runs.
        </p>
    </figure>

    <p>
        Here is the real code: an order form we paused both ways. One parameter picks how it asks for the
        pause: the .NET 10 way or the .NET 11 way.
    </p>

    <CodeFigure Number="3" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/CircuitPause/OrderDraft.razor" Feature="server-initiated-circuit-pause">
        <Caption>
            Three blocks are highlighted, and only the last is the fix. The <code>[PersistentState]</code> properties
            are what the tab saves when it pauses. They work as they did in .NET 10. The <code>ViaJavaScript</code>
            branch asks the .NET 10 way, and its await never returns. The <code>else</code> branch asks the .NET 11
            way, with one call to <code>RequestCircuitPauseAsync</code>.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="4" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/CircuitPause/CircuitTracker.cs" Feature="server-initiated-circuit-pause">
        <Caption>
            Where the circuit comes from. Nothing highlighted here is new: circuit handlers have always been given
            their <code>Circuit</code>. Each tab gets its own tracker, so it never sees another visitor's tab.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <h2><small>The .NET 10 way</small>Restart anyway, or call into the browser</h2>
    <p>We tried three options on .NET 10, with the same order form and a real restart:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Three ways to restart in .NET 10, and what the tab did</span>
        <div class="flow">
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip"><span class="dim">// no pause: stop the process</span></pre>
                <span class="why">"Rejoining the server…", then "Rejoin failed", then a reload on the new process</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ the form comes back empty</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">await js.InvokeVoidAsync(
    "Blazor.pauseCircuit");</pre>
                <span class="why">the tab pauses and resumes with its data</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ the await never returns</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip"><span class="dim">// in App.razor, by hand</span>
window.remotePause =
    () =&gt; { Blazor.pauseCircuit(); };</pre>
                <span class="why">called from server code, it returns before the circuit closes; the tab pauses with its data</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ works, but it's a script to ship, and you don't learn whether the tab was asked</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p>
        Only the third option works, and it needs JavaScript you write and ship yourself. You can't use the new
        method instead: <code>RequestCircuitPauseAsync</code> doesn't exist in .NET 10, so the build fails.
    </p>

    <h2><small>.NET 11</small>A server call that returns</h2>
    <p>
        In .NET 11 you ask through the <code>Circuit</code>. It's a small gain, but a real one: no script to ship, a
        call that returns, and a <code>bool</code> that tells you if the request went out. Deploy code can ask every
        open tab, then give the browsers a few seconds to save before the process exits.
    </p>

    <h2><small>Good to know</small></h2>
    <ul class="gk">
        <li><b><code>true</code> isn't "paused".</b> It means the request went out, not that the tab has finished saving. To know when it has, watch for the circuit to close, as Fig. 4's <code>OnCircuitClosedAsync</code> does.</li>
        <li><b>Pausing every tab before a restart?</b> That's the real deploy case. The component in Fig. 3 asks only its own tab, so it never pauses another visitor. To ask every tab, keep a single list of open circuits: add each one in <code>OnCircuitOpenedAsync</code> and remove it in <code>OnCircuitClosedAsync</code>. When the app starts shutting down (<code>IHostApplicationLifetime.ApplicationStopping</code>), call <code>RequestCircuitPauseAsync</code> on each. Then give the browsers a few seconds to save, with a time limit, before the process exits.</li>
        <li><b>Keep your Data Protection keys.</b> The paused state waits in the browser, encrypted. The new server needs the same keys to read it.</li>
        <li><b>A page can finish its work first.</b> Before a tab pauses, Blazor runs any <code>onCircuitPausing</code> callback you've registered in the browser.</li>
    </ul>
</article>
